[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-903883c2-c07e-50f2-a202-1c38e08cadfc" "question-66c0358e-dcf2-5965-acb4-eb43f1da9799" "question-14def4d3-3d45-5610-862f-8d4c651f0955"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-903883c2-c07e-50f2-a202-1c38e08cadfc" :question/id #uuid "903883c2-c07e-50f2-a202-1c38e08cadfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-32550" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-808d2fc9-2e7f-5ef1-86ff-cfcf9e8e2abc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-808d2fc9-2e7f-5ef1-86ff-cfcf9e8e2abc" :answer-field/id #uuid "808d2fc9-2e7f-5ef1-86ff-cfcf9e8e2abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5d903dcf-e2e4-575c-918f-1685ca2aa827" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d13